    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer; all devices participate in every forward pass.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts*; tokens are routed to whichever device owns the expert that the router selected.\n\nThese are orthogonal and frequently combined: TP shards dense attention/MLP blocks, while EP shards the MoE feed-forward layers. A third axis, **data parallelism (DP)**, replicates the whole sharded model to increase batch throughput. This note covers the mechanics of TP and EP during *inference* (no optimizer state, no gradients, but KV-cache and batching dynamics matter).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer layer. The two matrix multiplies in an MLP are:\n\n```\nY = X \u00b7 W1        (column-parallel: split W1 along output dim)\nZ = Y \u00b7 W2        (row-parallel:   split W2 along input dim)\n```\n\nWith TP degree `T` and rank `r \u2208 [0, T)`:\n\n- **Column-parallel linear** (`W1 \u2208 R^{d\u00d74d}` \u2192 `W1_r \u2208 R^{d\u00d74d/T}`): each rank computes `Y_r = X \u00b7 W1_r`. Input `X` is *replicated*; outputs are sharded along the feature dimension. No communication needed before the matmul.\n- **Row-parallel linear** (`W2 \u2208 R^{4d\u00d7d",
